@startuml
!pragma useVerticalIf on
title "RootCanal: How controller instances communicate with each other\n(in-memory broadcast through the virtual PhyLayer)"
scale 0.9

' ------------------------------------------------------------------
' Participants
' ------------------------------------------------------------------
actor "HOST A\n(Central/client)" as HA
actor "HOST B\n(Peripheral/server)" as HB
control "CPP Ctrl A\n(LeController)" as CA
control "CPP Ctrl B\n(LeController)" as CB
entity "PhyDevice A\n(addr X)" as PDA
entity "PhyDevice B\n(addr Y)" as PDB
entity "PhyLayer::BR_EDR\n/ PhyLayer::LOW_ENERGY" as PHY
entity "Sniffer Device\n(optional, in PHY)" as SNIF

note right of PHY
  PhyLayer holds a list of PhyDevice shared_ptrs
  registered on it. Two default phys are created in
  SetUpTestChannel(): "BR_EDR" and "LOW_ENERGY".
  A device/controller can be on several phys at once.

  PhyLayer::Send() broadcasts the packet to EVERY
  device except the sender (so all "listeners",
  including sniffers, see it).

  RSSI between two devices is fabricated by
  ComputeRssi() (no geometry by default).
end note

HA -> CA: HCI_LE_Create_Connection (over HCI channel)
activate CA
CA -> CA: HandleCommand -> LeCreateConnection\n(allocate ACL handle, remember state)
CA -> PDA: SendLinkLayerPacket(LeConnect{src:X, dst:Y})\n   -> PhyDevice::Send(data, LOW_ENERGY, tx_power)
activate PDA
PDA -> PHY: PhyLayer::Send(packet, tx_power, sender_id=idevA)
activate PHY

' broadcast fan-out
PHY -> PDB: PhyDevice::Receive(packet, LOW_ENERGY, rssi)\n   (PhyDevice::Receive -> device_->ReceiveLinkLayerPacket)
PHY -> SNIF: (optional) Sniffer::ReceiveLinkLayerPacket -> INFO log
deactivate PDA

activate PDB
PDB -> CB: CTRL_B.ReceiveLinkLayerPacket(LeConnect, rssi)
activate CB
CB -> CB: Handle as incoming connect request,\nprepare response, then:
CB -> PDB: SendLinkLayerPacket(LeConnectComplete{src:Y, dst:X})
activate PDB
PDB -> PHY: PhyLayer::Send(...)  (back the other way)
PHY -> PDA: PhyDevice::Receive -> CA.ReceiveLinkLayerPacket
deactivate PDB
deactivate PHY
deactivate PDB

CA -> CA: HandleCon/SetupLeAcl,\nemit HCI_LE_Connection_Complete to HOST A
CA -> HA: HCI event
deactivate CB

== Once the two controllers have a direct ACL link ==

note right of CA
  For BLE, an established ACL connection between the two
  controllers is emulated at the PhyLayer level by the
  LLCP packets (the Rust link layer's LLCP state machine,
  e.g. LL_VERSION_IND / LL_FEATURE_REQ etc.), and the ACL
  data frames sent by the hosts ride along as "ACL" type
  link layer packets.
end note

HA -> CA: ACL data (from host A's client)
CA -> PDA: SendLinkLayerPacket(Acl{src:X, dst:Y, acl_payload})
PDA -> PHY: PhyLayer::Send (LOW_ENERGY)
PHY -> PDB: Receive -> CB.ReceiveLinkLayerPacket
CB -> HB: HandleAcl -> sends ACL data to HOST B

' BR/EDR path uses LMP packets through the Rust LinkManager
note right of CA
  On the BR/EDR PhyLayer, controllers exchange LMP packets
  (PAGE, PAGE_RESPONSE, LMP procedures) — these are shaped
  by the Rust LinkManager (rust/src/lmp) and reach the air
  via the same PhyDevice -> PhyLayer -> broadcast route.
end note

== A packet is always broadcast, never unicast ==

note right of PHY
  There is no direct socket between controller A and
  controller B. Sending a link-layer packet from one
  controller simply calls into the shared PhyLayer which
  iterates its device list and delivers a copy to every
  registered PhyDevice (except the originator).
end note

@enduml